/**
 * NIGHTSHIFT — the player's hero car (visual only).
 *
 * Asset: public/assets/models/player_car.glb, generated by this project: modelled from code in Blender by
 * tools/car/build_car.py, then validated and compressed by tools/car/pack.mjs (contract: docs/assets/car.md).
 * The file is already in car space (Y up, faces -Z, origin = centre of the wheelbase on the ground) and split
 * into rigid parts: Body, MirrorL/R, Steering, Wheel_XX_Spin (rim + tyre + disc), Wheel_XX_Caliper.
 * It carries no textures: materials are looked up by name and every texture (paint flake, carbon weave,
 * rain beads, hexagonal metal mesh) is made here in code; fine patterns fade to their far-away look with
 * their screen size (shader patches below). TEXCOORD_0 is a box projection in metres (1 UV unit = 1 m); an optional
 * COLOR_0 is baked ambient occlusion (grey, 1 = open).
 *
 * Draw calls: instead of one mesh per (part × material) every material becomes ONE SkinnedMesh whose
 * vertices are rigidly bound (weight 1) to the bone of the part they belong to.
 * Bones = body, mirrors, steering wheel, 4 wheel spin groups, 4 calipers → one draw call per material,
 * independent of how the parts move. Skinning 12 bones is trivially cheap on the GPU.
 *
 * Hierarchy (all objects on LAYERS.PLAYER):
 *   root ─ attitude (pivot for pitch / roll at ATTITUDE_PIVOT_Y) ─ body ─ bones: body, mirrors, steering
 *        │                                                            └ anchors (lights, exhausts, hoodCam)
 *        ├ hub FL/FR/RL/RR (fixed on the ground; fronts yaw with steerAngle) ─ spin bone, caliper bone
 *        └ one SkinnedMesh per material
 */
